"""Diverse-prototype head — makes prototypes of a class specialise onto DISTINCT
structures, instead of all finding the same thing.

This is a NEW head. `pxai/models/protopnet.py` is untouched, so every existing result
remains reproducible and the original stays available for the thesis.

===============================================================================
THE PROBLEM
===============================================================================
In figs/protograd_overview.png the three highest-weighted prototypes of a class have
near-identical weights (1.23, 1.19, 1.00) and near-identical pixel-level attribution
maps. They are redundant: the class effectively has one prototype, not five.

Reading the original loss shows why:

    cluster = -(sim_pooled * oh).max(1).values.mean()     # max over prototypes
    sep     =  (sim_pooled * (1 - oh)).max(1).values.mean()
    l1      =  (self.last.weight * off).abs().sum()

**No term depends on two prototypes jointly.** `cluster` is a max, so only the single
best-matching prototype of the correct class receives gradient — the other k-1 get
nothing from it. `sep` is a max. `l1` is per-weight. Nothing pushes prototypes apart.

Meanwhile a force pulls them together: prototype push projects each prototype onto the
nearest same-class patch, and a trained embedding collapses same-class patches into a
tight region. Any k patches drawn from a tight cluster are similar. Diversity is
therefore accidental, and in practice absent.

===============================================================================
THE KEY STRUCTURAL FACT
===============================================================================
`add_on` ends in `nn.Sigmoid()`, so patch embeddings live in (0,1)^D — strictly
NON-NEGATIVE. After push, prototypes are patch embeddings and are non-negative too.

For non-negative vectors, cos(p, q) = 0 **requires disjoint support**.

So an orthogonality penalty here does not merely spread prototypes apart in some
abstract sense: it forces them onto DISJOINT FEATURE CHANNELS. Different channels of a
CNN encode different visual properties — edge orientation, texture frequency, local
contrast — so disjoint channels is the mechanical meaning of "responds to a different
structure". That is exactly the property we want and it comes free from the existing
architecture.

===============================================================================
FOUR MECHANISMS, INDEPENDENTLY SWITCHABLE
===============================================================================
Each is off by default, so `kind: protopnet_diverse` with no options reproduces the
original head. Turn them on one at a time; the right experiment is an ablation.

--- 1. ORTHOGONALITY  (`w_orth`) -----------------------------------------------
Penalise pairwise cosine similarity between prototypes of the SAME class:

    L_orth = mean over classes, over pairs (p,q), of cos(p,q)^2

Squared so it is smooth at zero and does not reward negative cosine (which is
unreachable for non-negative vectors anyway). Between-class pairs are left alone —
`sep` already handles those, and penalising them would fight it.

Precedent: Deformable ProtoPNet (Donnelly et al., 2022) uses an orthogonality loss on
same-class prototypes; TesNet (Wang et al., 2021) enforces orthonormal class bases on a
Grassmann manifold. This is the cheap version of that idea.

--- 2. CHANNEL SPARSITY  (`w_sparse`) ------------------------------------------
    L_sparse = mean |p| / mean over channels

L1 on the prototype vectors, normalised so it does not simply shrink everything. On its
own this concentrates each prototype on a few channels; TOGETHER WITH ORTHOGONALITY it
forces the supports to be not just disjoint but *small and* disjoint, which is a much
stronger form of specialisation. Orthogonality says "don't share channels"; sparsity
says "use few", and the pair says "each prototype owns a distinct handful of features".

--- 3. FOCAL SIMILARITY  (`focal`) ---------------------------------------------
Replace the pooled similarity

    sim_pooled[p] = max_ij sim[p,i,j]
with
    sim_pooled[p] = max_ij sim[p,i,j] - mean_ij sim[p,i,j]

A prototype now scores only if it matches ONE region much better than it matches the
image on average. This penalises "generically similar everywhere" prototypes directly.

It also addresses a defect measured independently: sim = log((d+1)/(d+eps)) is bounded
and non-zero everywhere, so the similarity maps carry a high floor — which is precisely
why the native attribution's mass concentration sat near 1.0 while its peak was correct.
Focal similarity subtracts that floor at the source.

Precedent: ProtoPool (Rymarczyk et al., 2022) introduces focal similarity for the same
reason — to stop prototypes activating on large uniform regions.

NOTE: this changes the forward pass, so a focal model is NOT weight-compatible with a
standard checkpoint. It must be trained from scratch.

--- 4. DIVERSE PUSH  (`diverse_push`, used by train.py) ------------------------
Standard push projects each prototype independently onto its nearest same-class patch,
so k prototypes can and do collapse onto the same patch. `assign_diverse` instead solves
a linear assignment (Hungarian) over a candidate pool, forcing the k prototypes of a
class onto k DISTINCT patches while minimising total distance.

Candidates are additionally de-duplicated by (image, spatial cell) so two prototypes
cannot take the same location of the same image.

===============================================================================
WHAT SUCCESS LOOKS LIKE, AND THE FAILURE MODE TO WATCH
===============================================================================
Measure with `probe_prototype_diversity.py`:

    DOWN: cos, jacc, map_corr        UP: eff (effective prototypes per class)

**`cells` should stay small.** The egg covers 1-2 cells of a 7x7 grid. Forcing
prototypes onto different CELLS would push most of them onto background and destroy the
localisation recovered in Part II. The goal is diversity WITHIN the object — same
region, different structures, therefore different channels and different pixel maps.

A rise in `cells` together with a fall in `conc_pos` means the diversity pressure is too
strong and is trading localisation for spread. Reduce `w_orth`.

===============================================================================
SUGGESTED ABLATION
===============================================================================
    A  w_orth=0    w_sparse=0    focal=False    -> reproduces the original
    B  w_orth=0.1  w_sparse=0    focal=False    -> orthogonality alone
    C  w_orth=0.1  w_sparse=0.01 focal=False    -> + sparsity
    D  w_orth=0.1  w_sparse=0.01 focal=True     -> + focal similarity
    E  as D, plus diverse_push=True             -> everything

Five runs, ~55 min each. Report cos/eff/jacc/map_corr AND conc_pos for every arm, so a
diversity gain bought with a localisation loss is visible rather than hidden.
"""
from __future__ import annotations

import torch
import torch.nn as nn
import torch.nn.functional as F


class DiverseProtoHead(nn.Module):
    """ProtoPNet head with explicit same-class prototype diversity.

    With all options at their defaults this is behaviourally identical to ProtoHead.
    """

    def __init__(self, in_channels: int, num_classes: int,
                 num_prototypes_per_class: int = 5, proto_dim: int = 128,
                 pip_sparsity: bool = True,
                 w_orth: float = 0.0, w_sparse: float = 0.0,
                 focal: bool = False, diverse_push: bool = False):
        super().__init__()
        self.num_classes = num_classes
        self.ppc = num_prototypes_per_class
        self.P = num_classes * num_prototypes_per_class
        # train.py's _push_prototypes reads head.num_protos; keep both names
        self.num_protos = self.P
        # train.py's _push_prototypes reads head.num_protos; keep both names
        self.num_protos = self.P
        self.proto_dim = proto_dim
        self.pip_sparsity = pip_sparsity
        self.w_orth = w_orth
        self.w_sparse = w_sparse
        self.focal = focal
        self.diverse_push = diverse_push

        self.add_on = nn.Sequential(
            nn.Conv2d(in_channels, proto_dim, 1), nn.ReLU(),
            nn.Conv2d(proto_dim, proto_dim, 1), nn.Sigmoid())     # -> (0,1)^D
        self.prototypes = nn.Parameter(torch.rand(self.P, proto_dim, 1, 1))

        pc = torch.zeros(self.P, num_classes)
        for p in range(self.P):
            pc[p, p // num_prototypes_per_class] = 1.0
        self.register_buffer("proto_class", pc)

        self.last = nn.Linear(self.P, num_classes, bias=False)
        with torch.no_grad():
            self.last.weight.copy_(pc.t())

    # ------------------------------------------------------------------ forward
    def _similarities(self, feat):
        z = self.add_on(feat)                                     # (B,D,H,W)
        B, D, H, W = z.shape
        zf = z.permute(0, 2, 3, 1).reshape(-1, D)                 # (B*H*W, D)
        pv = self.prototypes.view(self.P, D)
        d = (zf ** 2).sum(1, keepdim=True) - 2 * zf @ pv.t() + (pv ** 2).sum(1)
        d = d.clamp_min(0).view(B, H, W, self.P).permute(0, 3, 1, 2)
        sim = torch.log((d + 1.0) / (d + 1e-4))                   # (B,P,H,W)

        if self.focal:
            # max minus mean: score only what stands out from this image's baseline.
            # Removes the floor that makes the raw maps near-uniform.
            mx = F.max_pool2d(sim, sim.shape[-2:]).flatten(1)
            mn = sim.mean(dim=(-2, -1))
            pooled = mx - mn
        else:
            pooled = F.max_pool2d(sim, sim.shape[-2:]).flatten(1)
        return pooled, sim

    def forward(self, feat):
        pooled, _ = self._similarities(feat)
        w = F.relu(self.last.weight) if self.pip_sparsity else self.last.weight
        return F.linear(pooled, w)

    @torch.no_grad()
    def explain(self, feat):
        pooled, maps = self._similarities(feat)
        return {"sim_pooled": pooled, "sim_maps": maps,
                "proto_class": self.proto_class}

    # ------------------------------------------------------------ regularisers
    def cluster_sep_cost(self, feat, targets):
        """Unchanged from ProtoHead, so the diversity terms are the only difference."""
        pooled, _ = self._similarities(feat)
        oh = self.proto_class[:, targets].t()
        cluster = -(pooled * oh).max(1).values.mean()
        sep = (pooled * (1 - oh)).max(1).values.mean()
        return cluster, sep

    def l1_last(self):
        off = (1 - self.proto_class.t())
        return (self.last.weight * off).abs().sum()

    def orth_cost(self):
        """Mean squared cosine similarity between SAME-CLASS prototypes.

        Because embeddings (and, post-push, prototypes) are non-negative, driving this
        to zero forces disjoint channel support -- prototypes that respond to different
        feature dimensions, i.e. different visual structures.

        Between-class pairs are deliberately excluded: `sep` already separates classes,
        and penalising them here would fight it.
        """
        if self.w_orth <= 0 or self.ppc < 2:
            return self.prototypes.new_zeros(())
        pv = F.normalize(self.prototypes.view(self.P, self.proto_dim), dim=1)
        tot, n = pv.new_zeros(()), 0
        eye = torch.eye(self.ppc, device=pv.device)
        for c in range(self.num_classes):
            blk = pv[c * self.ppc:(c + 1) * self.ppc]
            g = blk @ blk.t()
            tot = tot + ((g - eye) ** 2).sum()
            n += self.ppc * (self.ppc - 1)
        return tot / max(n, 1)

    def sparse_cost(self):
        """Normalised L1 on prototype vectors.

        Normalising by the L2 norm means this concentrates mass on few channels rather
        than simply shrinking every prototype toward zero (which the distance term
        would then undo).
        """
        if self.w_sparse <= 0:
            return self.prototypes.new_zeros(())
        pv = self.prototypes.view(self.P, self.proto_dim)
        return (pv.abs().sum(1) / pv.norm(dim=1).clamp_min(1e-6)).mean()

    def diversity_cost(self):
        """Total diversity penalty. Add to the loss in train.py; zero when both
        weights are zero, so an unconfigured model is unchanged."""
        return self.w_orth * self.orth_cost() + self.w_sparse * self.sparse_cost()


# ------------------------------------------------------------------ diverse push
def assign_diverse(protos, cand, k):
    """Assign k prototypes to k DISTINCT candidate patches, minimising total distance.

    protos : (k, D)   the class's current prototypes
    cand   : (N, D)   candidate patches from that class (already de-duplicated by
                      image and spatial cell by the caller)
    returns: LongTensor (k,) of candidate indices, all distinct

    Standard push takes each prototype's nearest patch independently, so k prototypes
    can and do collapse onto one patch. A linear assignment forbids that while still
    minimising total cost. Falls back to greedy-without-replacement if scipy is absent.
    """
    if cand.shape[0] < k:
        return torch.arange(cand.shape[0])
    cost = torch.cdist(protos, cand).cpu().numpy()               # (k, N)
    try:
        from scipy.optimize import linear_sum_assignment
        _, col = linear_sum_assignment(cost)
        return torch.as_tensor(col, dtype=torch.long)
    except ImportError:
        taken, out = set(), []
        for r in range(k):
            order = cost[r].argsort()
            pick = next(int(i) for i in order if int(i) not in taken)
            taken.add(pick)
            out.append(pick)
        return torch.tensor(out, dtype=torch.long)
